  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "concept-recognition-biomedical-5f89",
  "summary": "Preprints and a published study now probe what protein and genomic language models learn beyond sequence prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is what sequence-trained language models internalize beyond next-token prediction. Protein language model embeddings encode a dominant nativeness axis that orders cellular, viral, and shuffled sequences. TaxoFormer extends this direction by pairing ESM-2 with an autoregressive decoder over a compact tokenization of the NCBI taxonomy to predict full protein lineages. A pseudoperplexity analysis of ProtT5 tests memorization by contrasting seen and novel holdout proteins. The published study shows that protein language model PPI classifiers trained on multi-species datasets can exploit taxonomic shared origin as a shortcut, with same-species negative sampling bearing on that confound. In genomics, DNA language models are evaluated as arithmetic-coding compressors, with DNAGPT2 variants reaching 1.47 bpb on the T2T human genome. The new element is systematic examination of what these models encode, including taxonomy, nativeness, memorization, and shortcut learning, rather than downstream accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-06-15"
}
